    "stages": [
      {
        "branch": "whole",
        "query": "Our platform \"FieldRelay\" is a React + Node.js incident management dashboard used by on-call SRE teams at logistics companies — they pull it up on phones, tablets, and war-room TVs during outages. We need to add a new \"Severity Classification\" feature: when an incident is created, the user picks from SEV1 through SEV5 using a guided wizard that walks them through impact questions (customer-facing downtime, data loss risk, blast radius, revenue impact per minute, etc.) and auto-suggests a severity level with required response SLAs, escalation chains, and communication templates for each level. The wizard and the resulting incident cards need to work flawlessly across devices — right now our layout completely breaks below 768px, the touch targets on the severity selector are way too small on mobile, and the real-time incident ticker on the war-room TV view doesn't scale to ultra-wide displays. I want you to plan this out spec-first: draft a detailed implementation spec covering the severity classification logic (all five levels with clear definitions, response time requirements, escalation rules, and when to upgrade/downgrade), the responsive redesign using a proper breakpoint system with container queries for the incident cards, fluid typography so the war-room view is readable from across the room, and accessible touch targets for the mobile on-call experience. Then walk me through it for review before we start building anything — I want to approve the spec, review each implementation phase, and make sure we get clean atomic commits at each stage.",
